"SetSecurityInfo function (aclapi.h) - Win32 apps | Microsoft Learn (https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/api/aclapi/nf-aclapi-setsecurityinfo)\nciteturn38view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/api/aclapi/nf-aclapi-setsecurityinfo\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 161\nL0: 【0†Skip to main content】 【1†Skip to Ask Learn chat experience】\nL1: \nL2: This browser is no longer supported.\nL3: \nL4: Upgrade to Microsoft Edge to take advantage of the latest features, security updates, and technical support.\nL5: \nL6: 【2†Download Microsoft Edge†go.microsoft.com】 【3†More info about Internet Explorer and Microsoft Edge】\nL7: \nL8: Table of contents Exit editor mode\nL9: \nL10: Ask Learn Ask Learn\nL11: \nL12: Reading mode Table of contents 【1†Read in English】 Add Add to Plans 【4†Edit†github.com】\nL13: \nL14: * * *\nL15: \nL16: Copy Markdown Print\nL17: \nL18: * * *\nL19: \nL20: Note\nL21: Access to this page requires authorization. You can try 【1†signing in】 or changing directories.\nL22: \nL23: Access to this page requires authorization. You can try changing directories.\nL24: # SetSecurityInfo function (aclapi.h)\nL25: \nL26: Feedback\nL27: \nL28: Summarize this article for me\nL29: \nL30: ## In this article\nL31: \nL32: The SetSecurityInfo function sets specified security information in the 【5†security descriptor】 of a specified object. The caller identifies the object by a handle.\nL33: \nL34: To set the SACL of an object, the caller must have the SE_SECURITY_NAME privilege enabled.\nL35: ## Syntax\nL36: \nL37:     `DWORD SetSecurityInfo(\nL38:       [in]           HANDLE               handle,\nL39:       [in]           SE_OBJECT_TYPE       ObjectType,\nL40:       [in]           SECURITY_INFORMATION SecurityInfo,\nL41:       [in, optional] PSID                 psidOwner,\nL42:       [in, optional] PSID                 psidGroup,\nL43:       [in, optional] PACL                 pDacl,\nL44:       [in, optional] PACL                 pSacl\nL45:     );\nL46:     `\nL47: ## Parameters\nL48: \nL49: `[in] handle`\nL50: \nL51: A handle to the object for which to set security information.\nL52: \nL53: `[in] ObjectType`\nL54: \nL55: A member of the 【6†SE_OBJECT_TYPE】 enumeration that indicates the type of object identified by the handle parameter.\nL56: \nL57: `[in] SecurityInfo`\nL58: \nL59: A set of bit flags that indicate the type of security information to set. This parameter can be a combination of the 【7†SECURITY_INFORMATION】 bit flags.\nL60: \nL61: `[in, optional] psidOwner`\nL62: A pointer to a SID that identifies the owner of the object. The SID must be one that can be assigned as the owner SID of a security descriptor. The SecurityInfo parameter must include the OWNER_SECURITY_INFORMATION flag. This parameter can be NULL if you are not setting the owner SID.\nL63: \nL64: `[in, optional] psidGroup`\nL65: A pointer to a SID that identifies the primary group of the object. The SecurityInfo parameter must include the GROUP_SECURITY_INFORMATION flag. This parameter can be NULL if you are not setting the primary group SID.\nL66: \nL67: `[in, optional] pDacl`\nL68: A pointer to the new DACL for the object. This parameter is ignored unless the value of the SecurityInfo parameter includes the DACL_SECURITY_INFORMATION flag. If the value of the SecurityInfo parameter includes the DACL_SECURITY_INFORMATION flag and the value of this parameter is set to NULL, full access to the object is granted to everyone. For information about null DACLs, see 【8†Creating a DACL】.\nL69: \nL70: `[in, optional] pSacl`\nL71: A pointer to the new SACL for the object. The SecurityInfo parameter must include any of the following flags: SACL_SECURITY_INFORMATION, LABEL_SECURITY_INFORMATION, ATTRIBUTE_SECURITY_INFORMATION, SCOPE_SECURITY_INFORMATION, or BACKUP_SECURITY_INFORMATION. If setting SACL_SECURITY_INFORMATION or SCOPE_SECURITY_INFORMATION, the caller must have the SE_SECURITY_NAME privilege enabled. This parameter can be NULL if you are not setting the SACL.\nL72: ## Return value\nL73: \nL74: If the function succeeds, the function returns ERROR_SUCCESS.\nL75: \nL76: If the function fails, it returns a nonzero error code defined in WinError.h.\nL77: ## Remarks\nL78: \nL79: If you are setting the 【9†discretionary access control list】 (DACL) or any elements in the 【5†system access control list】 (SACL) of an object, the system automatically propagates any inheritable 【10†access control entries】 (ACEs) to existing child objects, according to the 【11†ACE inheritance rules】.\nL80: \nL81: You can use the SetSecurityInfo function with the following types of objects:\nL82:   * Local or remote files or directories on an NTFS\nL83:   * Named pipes\nL84:   * Local or remote printers\nL85:   * Local or remote Windows services\nL86:   * Network shares\nL87:   * Registry keys\nL88:   * Semaphores, events, mutexes, and waitable timers\nL89:   * Processes, threads, jobs, and file-mapping objects\nL90:   * Window stations and desktops\nL91:   * Directory service objects\nL92: The SetSecurityInfo function does not reorder access-allowed or access-denied ACEs based on the preferred order. When propagating inheritable ACEs to existing child objects, SetSecurityInfo puts inherited ACEs in order after all of the noninherited ACEs in the DACLs of the child objects.\nL93: Note  If share access to the children of the object is not available, this function will not propagate unprotected ACEs to the children. For example, if a directory is opened with exclusive access, the operating system will not propagate unprotected ACEs to the subdirectories or files of that directory when the security on the directory is changed.\nL94: Warning  If the supplied handle was opened with an 【12†ACCESS_MASK】 value of MAXIMUM_ALLOWED, then the SetSecurityInfo function will not propagate ACEs to children.\nL95: ## Requirements\nL96: \nL97: Requirement  | Value\nL98: --- | ---\nL99: Minimum supported client  | Windows XP [desktop apps | UWP apps]\nL100: Minimum supported server  | Windows Server 2003 [desktop apps | UWP apps]\nL101: Target Platform  | Windows\nL102: Header  | aclapi.h\nL103: Library  | Advapi32.lib\nL104: DLL  | Advapi32.dll\nL105: ## See also\nL106: \nL107: 【13†ACL】\nL108: \nL109: 【14†Access Control】\nL110: \nL111: 【15†Basic Access Control Functions】\nL112: \nL113: 【16†GetNamedSecurityInfo】\nL114: \nL115: 【17†GetSecurityInfo】\nL116: \nL117: 【18†SECURITY_DESCRIPTOR】\nL118: \nL119: 【7†SECURITY_INFORMATION】\nL120: \nL121: 【6†SE_OBJECT_TYPE】\nL122: \nL123: 【19†SID】\nL124: \nL125: 【20†SetNamedSecurityInfo】\nL126: \nL127: * * *\nL128: \nL129: ## Feedback\nL130: \nL131: Was this page helpful?\nL132: \nL133: Yes No No\nL134: \nL135: Need help with this topic?\nL136: \nL137: Want to try using Ask Learn to clarify or guide you through this topic?\nL138: \nL139: Ask Learn Ask Learn\nL140: \nL141: Suggest a fix?\nL142: \nL143: * * *\nL144: \nL145: ## Additional resources\nL146: \nL147: * * *\nL148: \nL149:   * Last updated on 2021-10-13\nL150: ### In this article\nL151: \nL152: Was this page helpful?\nL153: \nL154: Need help with this topic?\nL155: \nL156: Want to try using Ask Learn to clarify or guide you through this topic?\nL157: \nL158: Ask Learn Ask Learn\nL159: \nL160: Suggest a fix?\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nAdjustTokenGroups function (securitybaseapi.h) - Win32 apps | Microsoft Learn (https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/api/securitybaseapi/nf-securitybaseapi-adjusttokengroups)\nciteturn38view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/api/securitybaseapi/nf-securitybaseapi-adjusttokengroups\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 143\nL0: 【0†Skip to main content】 【1†Skip to Ask Learn chat experience】\nL1: \nL2: This browser is no longer supported.\nL3: \nL4: Upgrade to Microsoft Edge to take advantage of the latest features, security updates, and technical support.\nL5: \nL6: 【2†Download Microsoft Edge†go.microsoft.com】 【3†More info about Internet Explorer and Microsoft Edge】\nL7: \nL8: Table of contents Exit editor mode\nL9: \nL10: Ask Learn Ask Learn\nL11: \nL12: Reading mode Table of contents 【1†Read in English】 Add Add to Plans 【4†Edit†github.com】\nL13: \nL14: * * *\nL15: \nL16: Copy Markdown Print\nL17: \nL18: * * *\nL19: \nL20: Note\nL21: Access to this page requires authorization. You can try 【1†signing in】 or changing directories.\nL22: \nL23: Access to this page requires authorization. You can try changing directories.\nL24: # AdjustTokenGroups function (securitybaseapi.h)\nL25: \nL26: Feedback\nL27: \nL28: Summarize this article for me\nL29: \nL30: ## In this article\nL31: \nL32: The AdjustTokenGroups function enables or disables groups already present in the specified 【5†access token】. Access to TOKEN_ADJUST_GROUPS is required to enable or disable groups in an access token.\nL33: ## Syntax\nL34: \nL35:     `BOOL AdjustTokenGroups(\nL36:       [in]            HANDLE        TokenHandle,\nL37:       [in]            BOOL          ResetToDefault,\nL38:       [in, optional]  PTOKEN_GROUPS NewState,\nL39:       [in]            DWORD         BufferLength,\nL40:       [out, optional] PTOKEN_GROUPS PreviousState,\nL41:       [out, optional] PDWORD        ReturnLength\nL42:     );\nL43:     `\nL44: ## Parameters\nL45: \nL46: `[in] TokenHandle`\nL47: \nL48: A handle to the access token that contains the groups to be enabled or disabled. The handle must have TOKEN_ADJUST_GROUPS access to the token. If the PreviousState parameter is not NULL, the handle must also have TOKEN_QUERY access.\nL49: \nL50: `[in] ResetToDefault`\nL51: Boolean value that indicates whether the groups are to be set to their default enabled and disabled states. If this value is TRUE, the groups are set to their default states and the NewState parameter is ignored. If this value is FALSE, the groups are set according to the information pointed to by the NewState parameter.\nL52: \nL53: `[in, optional] NewState`\nL54: A pointer to a 【6†TOKEN_GROUPS】 structure that contains the groups to be enabled or disabled. If the ResetToDefault parameter is FALSE, the function sets each of the groups to the value of that group's SE_GROUP_ENABLED attribute in the TOKEN_GROUPS structure. If ResetToDefault is TRUE, this parameter is ignored.\nL55: \nL56: `[in] BufferLength`\nL57: \nL58: The size, in bytes, of the buffer pointed to by the PreviousState parameter. This parameter can be zero if the PreviousState parameter is NULL.\nL59: `[out, optional] PreviousState`\nL60: \nL61: A pointer to a buffer that receives a 【6†TOKEN_GROUPS】 structure containing the previous state of any groups the function modifies. That is, if a group has been modified by this function, the group and its previous state are contained in the TOKEN_GROUPS structure referenced by PreviousState. If the GroupCount member of TOKEN_GROUPS is zero, then no groups have been changed by this function. This parameter can be NULL.\nL62: If a buffer is specified but it does not contain enough space to receive the complete list of modified groups, no group states are changed and the function fails. In this case, the function sets the variable pointed to by the ReturnLength parameter to the number of bytes required to hold the complete list of modified groups.\nL63: \nL64: `[out, optional] ReturnLength`\nL65: A pointer to a variable that receives the actual number of bytes needed for the buffer pointed to by the PreviousState parameter. This parameter can be NULL and is ignored if PreviousState is NULL.\nL66: ## Return value\nL67: \nL68: If the function succeeds, the return value is nonzero.\nL69: \nL70: If the function fails, the return value is zero. To get extended error information, call 【7†GetLastError】.\nL71: ## Remarks\nL72: \nL73: The information retrieved in the PreviousState parameter is formatted as a 【6†TOKEN_GROUPS】 structure. This means a pointer to the buffer can be passed as the NewState parameter in a subsequent call to the AdjustTokenGroups function, restoring the original state of the groups.\nL74: \nL75: The NewState parameter can list groups to be changed that are not present in the access token. This does not affect the successful modification of the groups in the token.\nL76: The AdjustTokenGroups function cannot disable groups with the SE_GROUP_MANDATORY attribute in the 【6†TOKEN_GROUPS】 structure. Use 【8†CreateRestrictedToken】 instead.\nL77: \nL78: You cannot enable a group that has the SE_GROUP_USE_FOR_DENY_ONLY attribute.\nL79: ## Requirements\nL80: \nL81: Requirement  | Value\nL82: --- | ---\nL83: Minimum supported client  | Windows XP [desktop apps | UWP apps]\nL84: Minimum supported server  | Windows Server 2003 [desktop apps | UWP apps]\nL85: Target Platform  | Windows\nL86: Header  | securitybaseapi.h (include Windows.h)\nL87: Library  | Advapi32.lib\nL88: DLL  | Advapi32.dll\nL89: ## See also\nL90: \nL91: 【9†Access Control Overview】\nL92: \nL93: 【10†AdjustTokenPrivileges】\nL94: \nL95: 【11†Basic Access Control Functions】\nL96: \nL97: 【8†CreateRestrictedToken】\nL98: \nL99: 【12†GetTokenInformation】\nL100: \nL101: 【13†OpenProcessToken】\nL102: \nL103: 【14†OpenThreadToken】\nL104: \nL105: 【15†SetTokenInformation】\nL106: \nL107: 【6†TOKEN_GROUPS】\nL108: \nL109: * * *\nL110: \nL111: ## Feedback\nL112: \nL113: Was this page helpful?\nL114: \nL115: Yes No No\nL116: \nL117: Need help with this topic?\nL118: \nL119: Want to try using Ask Learn to clarify or guide you through this topic?\nL120: \nL121: Ask Learn Ask Learn\nL122: \nL123: Suggest a fix?\nL124: \nL125: * * *\nL126: ## Additional resources\nL127: \nL128: * * *\nL129: \nL130:   * Last updated on 2021-10-13\nL131: \nL132: ### In this article\nL133: \nL134: Was this page helpful?\nL135: \nL136: Need help with this topic?\nL137: \nL138: Want to try using Ask Learn to clarify or guide you through this topic?\nL139: \nL140: Ask Learn Ask Learn\nL141: \nL142: Suggest a fix?\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nBypass traverse checking - Windows 10 | Microsoft Learn (https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/security/threat-protection/security-policy-settings/bypass-traverse-checking)\nciteturn38view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/security/threat-protection/security-policy-settings/bypass-traverse-checking\",\"lineno\":null}); Redirected to URL: https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/previous-versions/windows/it-pro/windows-10/security/threat-protection/security-policy-settings/bypass-traverse-checking; Total lines: 135\nL0: 【0†Skip to main content】 【1†Skip to Ask Learn chat experience】\nL1: \nL2: This browser is no longer supported.\nL3: \nL4: Upgrade to Microsoft Edge to take advantage of the latest features, security updates, and technical support.\nL5: \nL6: 【2†Download Microsoft Edge†go.microsoft.com】 【3†More info about Internet Explorer and Microsoft Edge】\nL7: \nL8: Table of contents Exit editor mode\nL9: \nL10: Ask Learn Ask Learn\nL11: \nL12: Reading mode Table of contents 【1†Read in English】 Add Add to Plans\nL13: \nL14: * * *\nL15: \nL16: Copy Markdown Print\nL17: \nL18: * * *\nL19: \nL20: Note\nL21: Access to this page requires authorization. You can try 【1†signing in】 or changing directories.\nL22: \nL23: Access to this page requires authorization. You can try changing directories.\nL24: # Bypass traverse checking\nL25: \nL26: Feedback\nL27: \nL28: Summarize this article for me\nL29: \nL30: ## In this article\nL31: \nL32: Applies to\nL33: \nL34:   * Windows 11\nL35:   * Windows 10\nL36: \nL37: > Learn more about what features and functionality are supported in each Windows edition at 【4†Compare Windows 10 Editions†www.microsoft.com】.\nL38: \nL39: Describes the best practices, location, values, policy management, and security considerations for the Bypass traverse checking security policy setting.\nL40: ## Reference\nL41: \nL42: This policy setting determines which users (or a process that acts on behalf of the user’s account) have permission to navigate an object path in the NTFS file system or in the registry without being checked for the Traverse Folder special access permission. This user right doesn't allow the user to list the contents of a folder. It only allows the user to traverse folders to access permitted files or subfolders.\nL43: \nL44: Constant: SeChangeNotifyPrivilege\nL45: ### Possible values\nL46: \nL47:   * User-defined list of accounts\nL48:   * Not Defined\nL49: \nL50: ### Best practices\nL51: \nL52:   1. Use access–based enumeration when you want to prevent users from seeing any folder or file to which they don't have access.\nL53:   2. Use the default settings of this policy in most cases. If you change the settings, verify your intent through testing.\nL54: \nL55: ### Location\nL56: \nL57: Computer Configuration\\Windows Settings\\Security Settings\\Local Policies\\User Rights Assignment\nL58: ### Default values\nL59: \nL60: The following table lists the actual and effective default policy values. Default values are also listed on the policy’s property page.\nL61: Server type or GPO  | Default value\nL62: --- | ---\nL63: Default Domain Policy  | Not Defined\nL64: Default Domain Controller Policy  | Administrators\nL65: Authenticated Users\nL66: Everyone\nL67: Local Service\nL68: Network Service\nL69: Pre-Windows 2000 Compatible Access\nL70: Stand-Alone Server Default Settings  | Administrators\nL71: Backup Operators\nL72: Users\nL73: Everyone\nL74: Local Service\nL75: Network Service\nL76: Domain Controller Effective Default Settings  | Administrators\nL77: Authenticated Users\nL78: Everyone\nL79: Local Service\nL80: Network Service\nL81: Pre-Windows 2000 Compatible Access\nL82: Member Server Effective Default Settings  | Administrators\nL83: Backup Operators\nL84: Users\nL85: Everyone\nL86: Local Service\nL87: Network Service\nL88: Client Computer Effective Default Settings  | Administrators\nL89: Backup Operators\nL90: Users\nL91: Everyone\nL92: Local Service\nL93: Network Service\nL94: ## Policy management\nL95: \nL96: Permissions to files and folders are controlled through the appropriate configuration of file system access control lists (ACLs). The ability to traverse the folder doesn't provide any Read or Write permissions to the user.\nL97: \nL98: A restart of the computer isn't required for this policy setting to be effective.\nL99: \nL100: Any change to the user rights assignment for an account becomes effective the next time the owner of the account logs on.\nL101: ### Group Policy\nL102: \nL103: Settings are applied in the following order through a Group Policy Object (GPO), which will overwrite settings on the local computer at the next Group Policy update:\nL104: \nL105:   1. Local policy settings\nL106:   2. Site policy settings\nL107:   3. Domain policy settings\nL108:   4. OU policy settings\nL109: \nL110: When a local setting is greyed out, it indicates that a GPO currently controls that setting.\nL111: ## Security considerations\nL112: \nL113: This section describes how an attacker might exploit a feature or its configuration, how to implement the countermeasure, and the possible negative consequences of countermeasure implementation.\nL114: ### Vulnerability\nL115: The default configuration for the Bypass traverse checking setting is to allow all users to bypass traverse checking. Permissions to files and folders are controlled through the appropriate configuration of file system access control lists (ACLs) because the ability to traverse the folder doesn't provide any Read or Write permissions to the user.\nL116: The only scenario in which the default configuration could lead to a mishap would be if the administrator who configures permissions doesn't understand how this policy setting works. For example, the administrator might expect that users who are unable to access a folder are unable to access the contents of any child folders. Such a situation is unlikely, and, therefore, this vulnerability presents little risk.\nL117: ### Countermeasure\nL118: Organizations that are concerned about security may want to remove the Everyone group from the list of groups that have the Bypass traverse checking user right. Taking explicit control over traversal assignments can be an effective way to limit access to sensitive information. Access–based enumeration can also be used. If you use access–based enumeration, users can't see any folder or file to which they don't have access. For more info about this feature, see 【5†Access-based Enumeration】.\nL119: ### Potential impact\nL120: The Windows operating systems and many applications were designed with the expectation that anyone who can legitimately access the computer will have this user right. Therefore, we recommend that you thoroughly test any changes to assignments of the Bypass traverse checking user right before you make such changes to production systems. In particular, IIS requires this user right to be assigned to the Network Service, Local Service, IIS_WPG, IUSR_<ComputerName>, and IWAM_<ComputerName> accounts.\nL121: (It must also be assigned to the ASPNET account through its membership in the Users group.) We recommend that you leave this policy setting at its default configuration.\nL122: ## Related topics\nL123: \nL124:   * 【6†User Rights Assignment】\nL125: \nL126: * * *\nL127: \nL128: ## Additional resources\nL129: \nL130: * * *\nL131: \nL132:   * Last updated on 2017-04-19\nL133: \nL134: ### In this article\n"
